"""Flavor texts: internal security and the Bundeswehr."""
D = {
"internal_security": "Student riots in the university towns, Palestinian commandos at the airports, and a new underground that calls itself the Red Army Faction and robs banks to finance a war against the 'imperialist state'. The federal police forces were built for another age - to catch smugglers and guard the border. The Republic must decide how to defend itself without becoming what its enemies say it is.",
"bka_expansion": "Horst Herold arrives at the Federal Criminal Police Office in Wiesbaden with a vision: police work as science, crime as data to be analysed, prevention rather than reaction. His budget multiplies, his staff triples, and the first mainframe computers are carried into the BKA's basements. The Bundestag gives him almost everything he asks for. Few ask what he will do with it.",
"inpol": "INPOL, the computerised wanted list, and PIOS - persons, institutions, objects, items - a database that links every fragment of information the police possess. The press calls Herold 'Kommissar Computer'. A patrol in Hamburg can now learn in seconds what a detective in Munich discovered last week. So can anyone else with access to the terminal.",
"dragnet": "Who pays his electricity bill in cash, rents a flat under a false name and never registers a car? The computer dragnet cross-matches the records of utilities, landlords and railways until only a handful of suspects remain. It is an extraordinary tool against terrorism - and it searches through the private lives of millions of innocent citizens to find one guilty one.",
"security_group": "The BKA's Security Group protects the constitutional organs: the federal president, the chancellor, the ministers. After the murders of Jürgen Ponto and Hanns Martin Schleyer the list grows to include bankers, judges, industrialists and diplomats - everyone the RAF might choose next. Armoured cars and bodyguards become the symbol of the German Autumn.",
"federal_property_protection": "The Federal Border Guard, created to defend the inner-German border, finds itself a new role: the protection of federal buildings, airports, embassies and nuclear installations. Its officers in green uniforms become a familiar sight at Frankfurt airport. Critics warn that the Federal Republic is building a national police force by the back door.",
"gsg9": "Fürstenfeldbruck, September 1972: the Bavarian police's rescue attempt ends with all nine Israeli hostages dead. Never again, says Interior Minister Genscher, and gives Ulrich Wegener the task of building a counter-terror unit inside the Federal Border Guard. Its name is deliberately boring: Border Guard Group 9. Its standards are not.",
"gsg9_training": "Wegener's men train with the British SAS at Hereford and with the Israeli special forces, test stun grenades, practise the storming of aircraft on disused runways and shoot by night at Hangelar. Every exercise is meant for one moment that may never come - and if it comes, there will be only a few seconds to get it right.",
"gsg9_global": "Mogadishu, October 1977: Lufthansa flight 181 is stormed in seven minutes and all eighty-six passengers are freed. GSG 9 becomes a legend overnight. Its mandate is now worldwide: wherever a German aircraft or embassy is held hostage, with the consent of the host country, the men from St. Augustin can go.",
"contact_ban_law": "Hanns Martin Schleyer has been kidnapped, and the crisis staff suspects that the RAF prisoners in Stammheim are directing the operation through their lawyers. The Contact Ban Act passes the Bundestag and the Bundesrat in a few days: prisoners can be cut off from all contact - even with their defence counsel. It is the fastest law in the Republic's history, and one of the most controversial.",
"stammheim_trial": "A fortified courtroom built next to the prison at Stuttgart-Stammheim, defence lawyers excluded for alleged complicity, the defendants on hunger strike in protest against their conditions of detention. Holger Meins has already starved to death in his cell. The trial of the RAF's leaders is meant to show the strength of the rule of law. The world watches whether it does.",
"paragraph_129a": "Membership of a terrorist organisation - or merely supporting or advertising one - becomes a crime of its own under Paragraph 129a of the Criminal Code. Prosecutors no longer have to prove who threw the bomb, only who belonged to the group. It is an effective weapon against the RAF. It also reaches publishers, printers and anyone who sprays a slogan on a wall.",
"strong_state": "Whoever attacks the state will meet the whole of the state: more police, more powers, more computers, and less patience with those who ask about the price. The government promises the citizens security. The intellectuals warn of a police state; the government answers that the real danger comes from those who shoot.",
"security_state": "Internment for terrorist sympathisers, an amendment to Article 102 to bring back the death penalty for hostage-takers, surveillance without a judge's warrant: in the crisis staff of 1977 such ideas were floated and quietly buried. Now they are written into law. The RAF wanted to provoke the state into revealing its 'fascist face'. Some will say it has succeeded.",
"radicals_decree": "Every applicant for the public service - teachers, train drivers, postmen - is checked for loyalty to the free democratic basic order. The Office for the Protection of the Constitution delivers its findings, and membership of the DKP can cost a young teacher her career. Three and a half million checks are carried out. Abroad, the word 'Berufsverbot' enters other languages.",
"no_radicals_decree": "No more routine vetting of every applicant, only individual examination where there is concrete evidence. Willy Brandt will later call the Radicals Decree one of the cardinal mistakes of his chancellorship. The government trusts the civil service - and trusts the young people who once marched in the streets to serve the state they criticised.",
"verfassungsschutz": "The HVA of Markus Wolf has hundreds of agents in the Federal Republic: secretaries in ministries, aides in the chancellery, officers in NATO headquarters. The Office for the Protection of the Constitution in Cologne needs more than files and filing cabinets. It needs agents of its own, defectors who talk, and the patience to turn the enemy's spies.",
"data_protection": "The Federal Data Protection Act of 1977, a data protection commissioner in Bonn, and in 1983 the Constitutional Court's census judgment: every citizen has the right to decide what the state may know about him. Informational self-determination becomes a fundamental right. The security services complain that their hands are tied. The citizens begin to read the small print.",
"liberal_state": "Gerhart Baum's answer to the German Autumn: a state that does not allow its enemies to dictate how unfree it becomes. Controls on the BKA's databases, limits on the dragnet, dialogue with the sympathisers' milieu instead of mass raids. The police unions call it naivety. The minister calls it the difference between a democracy and its enemies.",
"crown_witness": "Reduced sentences, perhaps even freedom, for terrorists who testify against their former comrades. Lawyers find the idea distasteful - justice sold for information - and the RAF's prisoners call every witness a traitor. But in the courtroom it works: for the first time, the prosecutors learn from the inside how the underground is organised.",
"exit_programme": "The Verfassungsschutz offers a way out of the underground: new identities, jobs, protection from former comrades and, where possible, leniency in court. Some of the members who left the RAF in the early 1980s are hiding in the GDR. Others are tired, frightened or simply disillusioned. A door that is open is sometimes more effective than one that is broken down.",
"reconciliation_offer": "January 1992: Justice Minister Klaus Kinkel offers the early release of RAF prisoners who renounce violence. The state reaches out its hand. In April the RAF answers with a statement that it will 'take back the escalation' and stop its attacks on leading figures of business and the state. It is not peace. It may be the beginning of the end.",
"militant_democracy": "The Basic Law has learned from Weimar: it protects itself. Parties that fight the free democratic order can be banned by the Constitutional Court, associations dissolved, and those who abuse their fundamental rights to destroy freedom can lose them. Militant democracy is the Republic's immune system. It must be used carefully - immune systems can attack the body they protect.",
"far_right_watch": "Wehrsportgruppen training in the woods, weapons caches buried in Lower Saxony, neo-Nazi rock concerts in village halls: for too long right-wing violence was counted as ordinary crime and the far right dismissed as a handful of old men and confused youths. The bomb at the Oktoberfest in 1980 ends that comfortable illusion. The state has to look to its right.",
"ban_wsg": "Karl-Heinz Hoffmann's Wehrsportgruppe trains in uniform in the Franconian woods with old army vehicles and weapons of uncertain origin, and Bavaria's interior ministry has long regarded it as a harmless collection of eccentrics. The federal interior minister disagrees. Banning it sends a signal - and drives some of its members into a darker underground.",
"after_rostock": "Rostock-Lichtenhagen, Mölln, Solingen: hostels set on fire while neighbours applaud, Turkish families burned to death in their homes. Hundreds of thousands form candle chains in every city, the government bans one neo-Nazi organisation after another, and the Republic slowly admits that its state looked away for too long. The candles are a beginning, not an answer.",
"ban_pkk": "Arson attacks on Turkish banks and travel agencies, the occupation of consulates, blockades of the motorways: the Kurdish conflict has come to Germany with the hundreds of thousands of Kurds who live here. In November 1993 the federal government bans the PKK. Ankara is grateful. Many Kurds in Germany feel that their cause has been criminalised along with the violence.",
"citizen_in_uniform": "Innere Führung - leadership from within: the Bundeswehr's founders wanted an army of citizens in uniform who obey the Basic Law before any order and know when an order must be refused. Today 495,000 soldiers, most of them conscripts, stand in twelve divisions along the inner-German border, every one of them assigned to NATO. It is the strongest conventional army in Western Europe - and it must never fight alone.",
"army_structure_3": "Army Structure 3 reorganises the field army into thirty-three brigades in twelve divisions, every one of them assigned to NATO's command in war. More armour, more mechanised infantry, more mobility: the Bundeswehr is to be able to meet the Warsaw Pact's tank armies at the border rather than retreat to the Rhine.",
"leber_modernisation": "Georg Leber, a bricklayer and trade unionist, becomes the most popular defence minister the Bundeswehr has ever had. He buys Leopard tanks, Marder infantry fighting vehicles and Gepard anti-aircraft guns, improves pay and barracks, and speaks to the conscripts as one working man to another. The soldiers trust him. That matters as much as the equipment.",
"leopard_2": "Krauss-Maffei's new main battle tank enters service in 1979: a 120-millimetre smoothbore gun, composite armour and a speed that astonishes NATO's observers. It is perhaps the best tank in the world, and suddenly everyone wants it - the Netherlands, Switzerland, Sweden, Spain. The Leopard 2 becomes the export success of the decade, and every sale abroad a political argument at home.",
"army_structure_4": "Thirty-six brigades, smaller but heavier, with more tanks and fewer men in each, and territorial forces of reservists to defend the rear. Army Structure 4 is designed for a Warsaw Pact attack with little warning. It is also designed for a demographic problem that the planners already see coming: there will soon not be enough young men.",
"conscription_w15": "Fifteen months of military service instead of eighteen: shorter service, more fairness, more young men called up rather than deferred. The conscripts are pleased, the generals less so. Every month cut from the period of service is a month of training lost - and a division that may not be ready when it is needed.",
"conscription_w18": "The baby boom is over. From 1987 the cohorts of eighteen-year-olds shrink year by year, and the Bundeswehr faces a choice: lengthen military service, or disband divisions that NATO's plans count on. Eighteen months of service is unpopular, expensive and - according to the defence ministry - unavoidable.",
"home_defence": "While the field army fights at the border, someone has to hold the rear: bridges, depots, airfields, the roads on which the American reinforcements will move. The Territorial Army's Heimatschutz brigades consist of reservists who train a few weeks a year and would be mobilised within days. They are the army of the second line. In a war, there may not be a first line for long.",
"army_of_unity": "The Bundeswehr takes over the National People's Army - an army trained for decades to fight it. The MiG-29 fighters are kept, the ships are sold, the tanks scrapped, and most of the officers dismissed. A few thousand East German soldiers are taken into the army of unity. For them, the citizen in uniform is a new idea; for the Bundeswehr, so are they.",
"army_structure_5": "370,000 soldiers for the whole of Germany, as promised to Gorbachev in the Caucasus and written into the Two Plus Four Treaty. Fewer tanks, fewer divisions, and new crisis reaction forces for a world in which the threat no longer stands at the inner-German border. The Bundeswehr shrinks and changes its mission in the same decade. It is not sure what the mission is.",
"tornado": "The Panavia Tornado is a European answer to an American question: Britain, Italy and Germany share the enormous cost of a low-level strike aircraft that can fly under the Warsaw Pact's radar and deliver conventional - and nuclear - weapons. It is late and expensive. It is also the first time Europe has built a front-line combat aircraft together.",
"bundeswehr_universities": "Officers who command conscripts with university degrees should have degrees themselves. The Bundeswehr universities in Hamburg and Munich give young officers an academic education - engineering, economics, education - alongside their military training. The traditionalists fear that the army will produce intellectuals. The reformers hope it will.",
"jaeger_90": "A European fighter for the 1990s, designed by Britain, Germany, Italy and Spain to replace the Phantom: expensive, late, plagued by disputes over work shares - and politically impossible to cancel, because too many jobs, too many ministries and too much European prestige depend on it. Every defence minister promises to cut it. None does.",
"patriot": "The Warsaw Pact's air armies could attack Germany's airfields in the first hours of a war. Patriot batteries from 1989, Roland and Hawk systems for lower altitudes, all integrated into NATO's air defence belt: a shield over the Federal Republic that the Soviets must take seriously. Each battery costs more than a hospital. The arithmetic of deterrence is expensive.",
"baltic_navy": "In a war, the Warsaw Pact's fleets would try to break out of the Baltic through the Danish straits. The Bundesmarine's fast attack craft, its small Type 206 submarines and its naval aviation are to stop them in the narrow waters off Fehmarn and Kiel. It is not a glamorous navy - no carriers, no cruisers - but in its waters it is the strongest in the West.",
"type_209": "HDW in Kiel builds a small, quiet, reliable diesel-electric submarine for export, and the world's navies queue up for it: Greece, Argentina, Peru, Turkey, India, South Korea. The Type 209 becomes the most successful submarine design of the post-war era. Every contract keeps the yard alive - and every contract is an argument about where German weapons should go.",
"f122_frigates": "Eight Bremen-class frigates for the North Sea and the Atlantic: the Bundesmarine leaves the narrow waters of the Baltic behind and takes its place among NATO's ocean-going escorts, protecting the convoys that would bring American reinforcements across the Atlantic in a war. The navy of a continental power learns to sail the open sea.",
"arms_export_guidelines": "No weapons into areas of tension: the guidelines of 1971 are strict - in principle. In practice every chancellor makes exceptions, for friends, for jobs, for strategic partners, and the exports quietly continue through licences, subsidiaries and partners abroad. The guidelines are a statement of conscience. Whether they are anything more depends on the government.",
"nato_awacs": "NATO's own fleet of airborne early-warning aircraft - flying radar stations that can see a hundred kilometres beyond the border - is based at Geilenkirchen, near Aachen, with German crews aboard alongside the Americans, Dutch and Italians. It is the first truly integrated NATO force. Germany pays a large share and gets a seat in the cockpit.",
"nuclear_sharing_role": "Pershing Ia missiles, Lance rockets and Tornado bombers at Büchel: the delivery systems are German, the crews are German, the warheads are American and stay under American control until the President releases them. Nuclear participation gives Bonn a voice in NATO's nuclear planning - and makes the Federal Republic a target in every Soviet war plan.",
"national_option": "The Federal Republic signed the Non-Proliferation Treaty, but it never quite closed the door. A fast breeder, a reprocessing plant, enriched uranium, engineers who know what they are doing: the components of a national nuclear option exist. Using them would be the greatest scandal in the Republic's history - and would end the Western alliance as Germany has known it.",
"forward_defence": "Not a metre of German soil is to be given up: NATO's strategy of forward defence means fighting at the inner-German border from the first hour rather than retreating to the Rhine. The Bundeswehr's corps dig in along the border, and their exercises are meant to be seen from the other side. Deterrence, after all, only works if the enemy believes in it.",
"prepare_defence_case": "The emergency laws of 1968, the Joint Committee that replaces parliament in war, mobilisation plans for 1.3 million reservists, requisition orders for every lorry in the country: the state of defence has been written down in detail and rehearsed in secret. Nobody wants it. Everybody in the ministry knows what to do if it comes.",
"civilian_service": "Conscientious objectors staff the hospitals, the care homes and the ambulances: 3,900 in 1961, more than a hundred thousand a generation later. The examination of conscience before a tribunal is abolished, the service extended to discourage abuse. Without the young men who refused the rifle, the German welfare state would no longer function.",
"out_of_area_ruling": "July 1994: the Constitutional Court in Karlsruhe rules that the Basic Law permits armed deployments of the Bundeswehr abroad within a system of collective security - provided the Bundestag approves each one. The 'parliamentary army' is born. The debate about whether Germany should send soldiers into the world's wars is over. The debate about which wars has only just begun.",
"kosovo_decision": "'Never again war', the Greens have always said. 'Never again Auschwitz', answers their foreign minister, Joschka Fischer, as Serbian forces drive the Kosovo Albanians from their homes. Without a UN mandate, the Luftwaffe's Tornados take part in NATO's air war - the first German combat mission since 1945, decided by a red-green government.",
"tornado_ecr": "Electronic combat and reconnaissance Tornados, armed with HARM missiles that home in on enemy radar: Germany's contribution to NATO air campaigns is not to drop bombs but to blind the enemy's air defence so that others can. It is a specialised role that the allies badly need - and a way for Germany to fight without being seen to fight too much.",
"npg_seat": "Bonn has no bomb, but it has a voice. In NATO's Nuclear Planning Group German defence ministers sit at the table with their American and British colleagues and discuss target lists - many of them on German soil, East and West. The Federal Republic cannot decide how nuclear weapons are used. It can make sure it is never forgotten when they are discussed.",
"dual_key": "The Pershing Ia missiles at Geilenkirchen belong to the Luftwaffe; the warheads in their bunkers belong to the US Army. One key each, and both must turn before a missile can fly. The arrangement is meant to reassure everyone - Washington, Moscow, the German voter. It also raises a question that nobody wants to answer: who turns his key first?",
"chemical_free_zone": "In 1985 the SPD opposition and the SED drafted a treaty for a Central Europe free of chemical weapons - an opposition party negotiating with a foreign government, to the fury of the Kohl cabinet. Here the government itself makes it its policy: no chemical weapons in either German state, with inspections on both sides. Washington is not amused; Moscow is.",
"gronau_enrichment": "The uranium enrichment plant at Gronau, run with the British and the Dutch, is civilian - officially, and under international safeguards. A few more cascades of centrifuges, a few fewer inspectors, and the Federal Republic would be months away from weapons-grade uranium. Nobody in Bonn says this aloud. Everyone in Washington and Moscow knows it.",
"german_bomb": "The taboo of 1945 breaks. Engineers from Karlsruhe and Jülich, uranium from Gronau, a delivery system borrowed from NATO's own arsenals and a test site that does not yet exist. Every government from Washington to Moscow, from London to Paris, will ask the same question - and the answer will decide whether the Federal Republic remains a member of the Western world.",
"fulda_barrier": "The Fulda Gap is where NATO's planners expect the Soviet tank armies to attack: the shortest route to Frankfurt and the Rhine. Prepared demolition shafts in every bridge and road near the border, barrier materials in depots, plans to flood the valleys - and never, officially, a fortification, because a wall would admit that the border is permanent.",
"reforger": "Return of Forces to Germany: every autumn tens of thousands of American soldiers fly across the Atlantic, collect their tanks from depots in the Rhineland and practise the defence of the border alongside the Bundeswehr. Fields are churned up, villages blocked by convoys, farmers compensated. It is the visible proof that the Americans will come. The Soviets watch every exercise.",
"wintex": "Every two years the federal government, the Joint Committee and NATO rehearse the state of defence for a fortnight in the government bunker under the Ahr valley - including the moment when NATO requests the release of nuclear weapons on targets in both German states. In 1989 the West German participants refuse to play that part of the script. The exercise is never held again.",
"civil_defence": "Shelters under schools and underground stations, sirens on every roof, iodine tablets in every town hall, emergency food stores in secret depots. Civil defence is meant to give the population a chance to survive a war. The peace movement calls it preparation for the unthinkable - and points out that there are shelters for only three per cent of the population.",
"peace_dividend_defence": "'Structural inability to attack': fewer tanks, more anti-tank infantry, minefields and barriers - an army so clearly designed for defence that the other side cannot read it as a threat. The idea comes from the peace research institutes and the SPD's left wing. The generals call it disarmament in disguise. The Soviets find it very interesting.",
}
